<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Section 2.4: Sinusoidal Functions</title>

  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  <p workspace="0.40in">
    Recall transformations of a function <m>f(x)</m>, make sure you know what
    <m>a, b,</m> and <m>c</m> do to <m>f(x)</m> below:
    <md>
      g(x) = af(x-b)+c
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    For the normal standard functions <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m> and
    <m>g(t)=\cos(t)</m>, both are circular functions with the same values:
  </p>

  <sidebyside widths="35% 55%">
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            midline: <fillin/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            amplitude: <fillin/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            period: <fillin/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            range: <fillin/>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  

    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A blank graph to draw the sine and cosine functions.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-blank-soln">
        <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            points=['0','pi/6']
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" hlabels="(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>

  <p>
    The <q>starting point</q> is where the normal <m>\sin(t)</m> and
    <m>\cos(t)</m> functions start at <m>t=0</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          starting point for <m>\sin(t)</m>: <fillin/>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          starting point for <m>\cos(t)</m>: <fillin/>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Transformations of sine and cosine</title>

    <p>
      The real numbers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> with <m>a\ne 0</m>, we
      transform <m>\cos(t)</m> and <m>\sin(t)</m> by:
      <md>
        k(t) = a\cos(t-b)+c
  \text{   \ \ \  and  \ \ \   }  h(t) = a\sin(t-b)+c
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>horizontal shift</em> by <m>b</m> units to the right (if
            <m>b&gt;0</m> ),
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            then a <em>vertical stretch</em> by <m>|a|</m> units (if
            <m>a&lt;0</m>, we also reflect across the <m>y</m>-axis),
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            then by a <em>vertical shift</em> of <m>c</m> units.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      The resulting circular functions have
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            midline <m>y=c</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            amplitude <m>|a|</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            range <m>[c-|a|, c+|a|]</m>,
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            and period <m>2\pi</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The point <m>(b,a+c)</m> lies on the graph of <m>k</m> and the point
            <m>(b,c)</m> on <m>h</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The <term>anchor point</term> is where the starting point of the standard
    <m>\cos(t)</m> and <m>\sin(t)</m> functions shift under these
    transformations.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          the <term>anchor point</term> for <m>k(t) = a\cos(t-b)+c</m> (the
          shifted <m>\cos(t)</m> ) is: <fillin/>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          the <term>anchor point</term> for <m>h(t) = a\sin(t-b)+c</m> (the
          shifted <m>\sin(t)</m> ) is: <fillin/>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.4.2</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a spring-mass system where a weight is resting on a
        frictionless table.
        We let <m>d(t)</m> denote the distance from the wall (where the spring
        is attached) to the weight at time <m>t</m> in seconds and know that the
        weight oscillates periodically with a minimum value of <m>2</m> feet and
        a maximum value of <m>7</m> feet with a period of <m>2\pi</m>.
        We also know that <m>d(0)=4.5</m> and <m>d(\frac{\pi}{2}) = 2</m>.
      </p>

      <p workspace="3in">
        Determine a formula for <m>d(t)</m> in the form
        <m>d(t) = a\cos(t-b)+c</m> or <m>d(t) = a\sin(t-b)+c</m>.
        (It will help to start with identifying the key information, such as the
        midline, the amplitude, ...) Is it possible to find two different
        formulas that work?
        For any formula you find, identify the anchor point.
      </p>

     
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        Pick out the keypoints first.  The min and max values get us the range, 
        the average of the max and min get us the value of the midline, and the difference 
        between the max and midline is the amplitude.  And the period is given. So we have 
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[2,7]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=4.5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>2.5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              period <m>2\pi</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Now the two initial values tell us that we start on the midline, and 
        the first quarter of the whole period puts us at the bottom of the graph, or 
        in other words, if we choose <m>(0,4.5)</m> as our anchor point, the sine 
        function starts on the midline.  However the sine usually goes up first, we are 
        going down first, which means this is a flipped since, or <m>a\lt 0</m>.  So we have 
        <md>
          d(t) = a\sin(t-b)+c
        </md>
        with <m>a</m> being the amplitude (but negative since we flip the sine function) so 
        <m>a = -2.5</m>, <m>b</m> is the horizontal shift but we are starting at 
        <m>t=0</m> so we aren't shifting horizontally, and <m>c</m> is 
        the vertical shift which is the value of the midline, so <m>c = 4.5</m>. So a perfectly valid formula
        is 
        <md>
          d(t) = -2.5\sin(t-0)+4.5.
        </md>
        (And no, we don't need to write the <m>-0</m>!)
      </p>
      <p>
        It'll look something like this:

      </p>
        <image width="65%">
      <shortdescription>The graph of d(t).</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-2">
        <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>f(x)=2</definition>
          <definition>g(x)=7</definition>
          <definition>h(x)=4.5</definition>
          <definition>d(t)=-2.5*sin(t-0)+4.5</definition>
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1,2*pi+pi/4,8)">
            <axes  decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="d(t)" h-pi-format="yes" 
            hlabels="(0,pi/2,2*pi)" vlabels="(0,10,10)" />
          <graph function="f" dash="4 4"/>
          <graph function="g" dash="4 4"/>
          <graph function="h" dash="4 4"/>
          <graph function="d" />
          <point p="(0,d(0))"/>
          <point p="(pi/2,d(pi/2))"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      <p>
        But I could just as easily choose another anchor point.  If I wanted to 
        start at <m>(\frac\pi 2, 2)</m>, I'm starting at the minimum.  A flipped cosine 
        graph starts at the minimum.  My amplitude, period, and midline are the same, so 
        <m>a, c</m> don't change, but I've now shifted to the right by 
        <m>\frac\pi2</m>, so <m>b = \frac\pi2</m>.  So another perfectly 
        valid formula is 
        <md>d(t) = -2.5\cos(t-\frac\pi2)+4.5</md>
        (There are more anchor points we can choose!)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Horizontal scaling</title>
    <p>
      We squish or stretch the function in the horizontal direction around the y-axis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Given a function <m>y=f(t)</m> and a real number <m>k&gt;0</m>,
      <m>y=h(t)=f(kt)</m> is a <term>horizontal stretch</term> of the graph of
      <m>f</m>, that is
      <em>we multiply the <m>x-</m>coordinate by <m>\frac{1}{k}</m></em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The point <m>(t,f(t))</m> then stretches to the point
      <m>(\frac{1}{k}t, f(t))</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>0&lt;k&lt;1</m>, the graph of <m>h</m> is a stretch of <m>f</m> away
      from the <m>y</m>-axis by a factor of <m>\frac{1}{k}</m>; if
      <m>k&gt;1</m>, the graph of <m>h</m> is a compression of <m>f</m> toward
      the <m>y</m>-axis by a factor of <m>\frac{1}{k}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The only point on the graph of <m>f</m> that is unchanged by the
      transformation is <m>(0,f(0))</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.4.3</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> given ni the figure below (
        <m>f</m> on the left, <m>g</m> on the right).
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
        <image >
          <shortdescription>
            A function f for you to transform in the following activities
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3a">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
              <definition>
                f(t)=abs(t-2)-2
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
                <point p="(2,-2)" alignment="se">
                  <m>
                    (2,-2)
                  </m>
                </point>
                <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                <point p="(4,f(4))"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>

        <image >
          <shortdescription>
            A function g for you to transform in the following activities
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3b">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
              <definition>
                g(t)=-(t+2)^2+2
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="g(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                <graph function="g"/>
                <point p="(-2,g(-2))" alignment="nw">
                  <m>
                    (-2,2)
                  </m>
                </point>
                <point p="(0,g(0))"/>
                <point p="(-4,g(-4))"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.01in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=f(t)</m>, sketch the following
          graphs: <m>{y=h(t) = f(\frac{1}{3}t)}</m> and <m>{y=j(t) = f(4t)}</m>.
          Be sure to label several points on each of <m>f</m>, <m>h</m>, and
          <m>j</m> with arrows to indicate their correspondence.
          In addition, write one sentence to explain the overall transformations
          that have resulted in <m>h</m> and <m>j</m> from <m>f</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        <m>h(t)=f(\frac13 t)</m> is a horizontal stretch by a factor of <m>3</m>, so <m>(2,-2)</m> goes to the point <m>(6,-2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>j(t)=f(4 t)</m> is a horizontal stretch by a factor of <m>\frac14</m>, so <m>(2,-2)</m> goes to the point <m>(0.5,-2)</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="25%">
          <shortdescription>
            A function f stretched horizontally by a factor of 3 and by a factor of 1/4.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3a-soln">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
              <definition>
                f(t)=abs(t-2)-2
              </definition>
              <definition>h(t)=f(1/3*t)</definition>
              <definition>j(t)=f(4*t)</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
                <point p="(2,-2)" alignment="se" fill="blue">
                  <m>
                    (2,-2)
                  </m>
                </point>
                <point p="(0,f(0))" fill="blue"/>
                <point p="(4,f(4))" fill="blue"/>
                <graph function="h"  stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                <graph function="j"  stroke="orange" dash="14 14"/>
                <point p="(.5,j(.5))" fill="orange"/>
                <point p="(6,h(6))" fill="red" />
                <label anchor="(-6,1)"><m>h(t)</m></label>
                <label anchor="(-5,7)"><m>j(t)</m></label>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.01in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the same axes as the plot of <m>y=g(t)</m> above, sketch the
          following graphs: <m>{y=k(t)=g(2t)}</m> and
          <m>{y=m(t)=g(\frac{1}{2}t)}</m>.
          Be sure to label several points on each of <m>g</m>, <m>k</m>, and
          <m>m</m> with arrows to indicate their correspondence.
          In addition, write one sentence to explain the overall transformations
          that have resulted in <m>k</m> and <m>m</m> from <m>g</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
       <p>
        <m>k(t)=g(2 t)</m> is a horizontal stretch by a factor of <m>\frac12</m>, so <m>(-2,2)</m> goes to the point <m>(-1,2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>m(t)=g(\frac12 t)</m> is a horizontal stretch by a factor of <m>2</m>, so <m>(-2,2)</m> goes to the point <m>(-4,2)</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="25%">
          <shortdescription>
            A function g transformed by a horizontal stretch by a factor of 1/2 and by a factor of 2
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3b-soln">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
              <definition>
                g(t)=-(t+2)^2+2
              </definition>
              <definition>k(t)=g(2*t)</definition>
              <definition>m(t)=g(1/2*t)</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="g(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                <graph function="g"/>
                <point p="(-2,g(-2))" fill="blue"/>
                <point p="(0,g(0))" fill="blue"/>
                <point p="(-4,g(-4))" fill="blue"/>
                <graph function="k"  stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                <graph function="m"  stroke="orange" dash="14 14"/>
                <point p="(-4,m(-4))" fill="orange"/>
                <point p="(-1,k(-1))" fill="red" />
                <label anchor="(-1,2)" alignment="e"><m>k(t)</m></label>
                <label anchor="(-6,2)"><m>m(t)</m></label>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the additional copies of the two figures below, sketch the graphs
          of the following transformed functions:
          <m>{y=r(t) = 2f(\frac12 t)}</m> (at left) and
          <m>{y=s(t) = \frac12g(2t)}</m> (at right).
          As above, be sure to label several points on each graph and indicate
          their correspondence to points on the original parent function.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="30% 30%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A function f for you to transform in the following activities
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3a2">
              <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                <definition>
                  f(t)=abs(t-2)-2
                </definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                  <graph function="f"/>
                  <point p="(2,-2)" alignment="se">
                    <m>
                      (2,-2)
                    </m>
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                  <point p="(4,f(4))"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A function g for you to transform in the following activities
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3b2">
              <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                <definition>
                  g(t)=-(t+2)^2+2
                </definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="g(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                  <graph function="g"/>
                  <point p="(-2,g(-2))" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      (-2,2)
                    </m>
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,g(0))"/>
                  <point p="(-4,g(-4))"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        <m>r(t)</m> has both a horizontal and vertical stretch by a factor of 2, so we double both coordinates.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>s(t)</m> has both a horizontal and vertical stretch by a factor of 1/2, so we cut in half both coordinates.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="25% 25%">
        <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The function f(t) stretched vertically and horizontally by a factor of 2.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3a2-soln">
              <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                <definition>
                  f(t)=abs(t-2)-2
                </definition>
                <definition>r(t)=2*f(1/2*t)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                  <graph function="f"/>
                  <point p="(2,-2)" alignment="se">
                  
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                  <point p="(4,f(4))"/>
                  <graph function="r" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                  <point p="(4,-4)"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The function g(t) stretched vertically and horizontally by a factor of 1/2.

            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-4-3b2-soln">
              <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
                <definition>
                  g(t)=-(t+2)^2+2
                </definition>
                <definition>s(t)=1/2*g(2*t)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="yes" xlabel="t" ylabel="g(t)" spacings="((-8,2,8),(-8,2,8))" hlabels="(-6,6,6)" vlabels="(-6,6,6)"/>
                  <graph function="g"/>
                  <point p="(-2,g(-2))" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      (-2,2)
                    </m>
                  </point>
                  <point p="(0,g(0))"/>
                  <point p="(-4,g(-4))"/>
                  <graph function="s" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
                  <point p="(-1,1)"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.01in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe in words how the function <m>y=r(t) = 2f(\frac12 t)</m> is
          the result of composing two elementary transformations of
          <m>y=f(t)</m>.
          Does the order in which these transformations are composed matter?
          Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        One is an x-coordinate and the other a y-coordinate, so order does not matter here.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <assemblage>
    <title>The period of a circular function</title>

    <p>
      For any constant <m>k&gt;0</m>, the period of the functions
      <m>\sin(kt)</m> and <m>\cos(kt)</m> is
      <md>
        P = \frac{2\pi}{k}
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.4.4</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Determine the exact period, amplitude, and midline of each of the
        following functions.
        In addition, state the range of each function, any horizontal shift that
        has been introduced to the graph, and identify an anchor point.
        Make your conclusions <em>without consulting Desmos</em>, and then use
        the program to check your work.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>p(x) = \sin(10x)+2</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        (<m>k=10, a=1, b=0, c=2</m>)
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>\frac{2\pi}{10} = \frac{\pi}{5}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[1,3]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: none (<m>b=0</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(0,2)</m> since no horizontal shift and sine starts on the midline
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>q(x) = -3\cos(0.25x)-4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        (<m>k=0.25=\frac14, a=-3, b=0, c=-4</m>)
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>\frac{2\pi}{0.25} = \frac{2\pi}{1/4}=8\pi</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>3</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=-4</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[-7,-1]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: none (<m>b=0</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(0,-7)</m> since no horizontal shift and 
              flipped cosine starts on the bottom (and it's flipped because <m>a\lt 0</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>r(x) = 2\sin(\frac{\pi}{4}x)+5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        (<m>k=\frac\pi4, a=2, b=0, c=5</m>)
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>\frac{2\pi}{\pi/4} = \frac{2\pi}{1}\frac{4}{\pi} = 8</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[3,7]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: none (<m>b=0</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(0,5)</m> since no horizontal shift and 
              sine starts on the midline which is at <m>y=5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>w(x) = 2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(x-3)\right)+5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        (<m>k=\frac\pi2, a=2, b=3, c=5</m>)
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>\frac{2\pi}{\pi/2} = \frac{2\pi}{1}\frac{2}{\pi}=4</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>2</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[3,7]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: right 3 (<m>b=3</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(3,2)</m> since horizontal shift of 3
             and cosine starts at the top which is <m>7</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>u(x) = -0.25\sin(3x-6)+5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        Need a rewrite: <m>u(x) = -0.25\sin(3(x-2))+5</m>.  
        </p><p>
        (<m>k=3, a=-0.25, b=2, c=5</m>).
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude <m>0.25</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline <m>y=5</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              range <m>[4.75,5.25]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: right by 2 (<m>b=2</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(2,5)</m> since horizontal shift of 2 
              and sine starts on the midline.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p workspace="1in">
    If we are given the period of a sine or cosine function, we can rearrange
    the equation for the period <m>{P=\frac{2\pi}{k}}</m> to solve for the
    <m>k</m>.
    Do so; what is <m>k</m> equal to in terms of the period <m>P</m>?
  </p>

  <p workspace=".5cm">
    This will help us build a circular function, see the next activity.
  </p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.4.5</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a spring-mass system where the weight is hanging from the
        ceiling in such a way that the following is known: we let <m>d(t)</m>
        denote the distance from the ceiling to the weight at time <m>t</m> in
        seconds and know that the weight oscillates periodically with a minimum
        value of <m>1.5</m> feet and a maximum value of <m>4</m> feet, with a
        period of <m>3</m>, and you know <m>d(0.5) = 2.75</m> and
        <m>d(1.25) = 4</m>.
      </p>

      <p workspace="3in">
        State the midline, amplitude, range, and an anchor point for the
        function, and hence determine a formula for <m>d(t)</m> in the form
        <m>a\cos(k(t-b))+c</m> or <m>a\sin(k(t-b))+c</m>.
        Show your work and thinking, and use Desmos appropriately to check that
        your formula generates the desired behavior.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              range: <m>[1.5,4]</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              P = <m>3</m> which means that <m>k = \frac{2\pi}{P} = \frac{2\pi}{3}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              midline: halfway between max and min: <m>\frac{1.5+4}{2} = 2.75</m> so <m>y=2.75</m> is the midline
              and so <m>c = 2.75</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              amplitude: <m>4-2.75 = 1.25</m>, so <m>|a|=1.25</m> and <m>a=\pm 1.25</m> depending
              on if it flips or not
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              horizontal shift: since <m>d(0.5) = 2.75</m> which is on the midline, 
              you can take the horizontal shift to be <m>0.5</m> and have 
              your function be sine (since sine starts on the midline). In this case, 
              we then have <m>b=0.5</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              anchor point: <m>(0.5,2.75)</m> since horizontal shift of <m>0.5</m> 
              and sine starts on the midline.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Flipped or not flipped?  We have <m>d(0.5)</m> which is on the midline.
              The next point given is <m>d(1.25)=4</m> which is at the max of the range.
              Since the period is <m>3</m>, then one quarter of the period is 
              <m>\frac{3}4=0.75</m>, so <m>t=1.25</m> represents a quarter of the period 
              from the anchor point of <m>t=0.5</m>.  Then the function goes up first like normal 
              sine, not flipped since.  So <m>a = +1.25</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        All together now:
        <md>
          d(t) = +1.25\sin(\frac{2\pi}{3}(t-0.5))+2.75.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>
</handout>